This PR adds support for the safe head fast confirmation rule to the Prysm beacon node, behind the `--safe-head-fcu` feature flag. When enabled, Prysm runs the fast confirmation algorithm as described in [this spec](https://github.com/adiasg/eth2.0-specs/blob/3e3ef28ace725b7b883f1908189406c890a569c3/fork_choice/confirmation-rule.md).  The safe / fast confirmed head will be passed to the FCU execution API endpoint as the safe head which could be queried on the EL as field "safe".
 
For context and prerequisites, see the [ethresearch post](https://ethresear.ch/t/confirmation-rule-for-ethereum-pos/15454) and the [white paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.00549).

## Detail design:

### Fast confirmation timeline

At second 0 of slot `n`, in the happy case, the Prysm beacon node calls `UpdateHead` as regular interval, which will fast confirm the block at slot `n-1` after collecting, verifying, and counting attestations for it starting at second 4. So in the happy case, a block can be fast confirmed in under 12 seconds.  
If the block at `n-1` is not the head, it won't be fast confirmed and the fast confirmation head will remain the same as the previous head.

### Forkchoice object

The Forkchoice object now additionally caches the safe head root.

### Node object

The Node caches an alternate weight value that excludes the influence of proposer boost.

#### Node can get max possible weight

This function calculates the maximum possible support a node could have accumulated between its starting slot and a given current slot, based on the committee weight and slot-to-epoch boundaries. If the slot range is within a single epoch, it returns the total support as the product of committee weight and slot count. If the range spans multiple full epochs, it returns the maximum support as one full epoch's worth of committee weight. For ranges that cross into a new epoch but do not cover an entire epoch, it performs a proportional calculation based on the number of slots in each epoch segment.

#### Node can check if it can be fast confirmed

This function checks if a node is "confirmed" by comparing its vote-only weight to a calculated safety threshold. It first ensures the node's slot is earlier than the current slot, then computes the maximum possible support and proposer boost weight. The node is considered confirmed if its vote-only weight exceeds half the sum of maximum possible support and proposer boost.

$\text{voteOnlyWeight} > \frac{\text{maxPossibleSupport} + \text{proposerBoostWeight}}{2} + ConfirmationByzatineThreshold $

where:

$\text{proposerBoostWeight} = \frac{\text{committeeWeight} \times \text{ProposerScoreBoost}}{100}$

#### Get Head: Apply weight changes

To apply a weight change, we also pass in the current proposer boost root and the previous proposer boost score so the node can cache its weight without proposer boost for fast confirm score comparison

#### Get Head: Update best descendant changes

To update the best descendant, we also pass in the number of seconds into the slot and the committee weight of the current chain.
During the first interval of the slot, the function checks if the best child is confirmed using the fast confirmed rule above based on the previous slot. If confirmed, it recursively assigns `bestConfirmedDescendant` to the deepest confirmed node by following the child’s own `bestConfirmedDescendant`. Otherwise, it assigns the best child itself. If the best child is not confirmed, `bestConfirmedDescendant` is set to nil. This recursive logic ensures fast confirmation status propagates back to the ancestor node.
```go
if hasViableDescendant {
	...
	if secondsSinceSlotStart < params.BeaconConfig().SecondsPerSlot/params.BeaconConfig().IntervalsPerSlot {
		// Attestations from the current slot are not accounted for in the fork choice - so compute safe head on the basis of the previous slot
		if bestChild.isOneConfirmed(currentSlot-1, committeeWeight) {
			if bestChild.bestConfirmedDescendant == nil {
				n.bestConfirmedDescendant = bestChild
			} else {
				n.bestConfirmedDescendant = bestChild.bestConfirmedDescendant
			}
		} else {
			n.bestConfirmedDescendant = nil
		}
	}
```

Adds custom functions to create differences between beacon states, serialize them and apply them. 

This adds a package `hdiff` which exposes one type and two functions

```
type HdiffBytes struct {
	StateDiff      []byte
	ValidatorDiffs []byte
	BalancesDiff   []byte
}

func Diff(source, target state.ReadOnlyBeaconState) (HdiffBytes, error)
 
func ApplyDiff(ctx context.Context, source state.BeaconState, diff HdiffBytes) (state.BeaconState, error) 
```

The type is exposed and the members are accessible because in a future PR we will expose functions to apply differences only to the validator set and the balance slice. 

Current benchmarks for applications of a diff between a state 6 months ago in Deneb vs current Electra state point to 1.1" for deserialization and application of the diff, on AMD Ryzen 5 3600. Creation and serialization of the diff takes 530ms on this computer and produces a 27MB diff.

